# claude-brain

Personal memory system for Claude Code. Local SQLite + MCP + hooks.

> **This file is a TEMPLATE.** Copy to your own project's `CLAUDE.md` and edit
> to taste. The rules below are starting points — keep what fits, replace what
> doesn't. The original upstream version of this template prescribed a strict
> "GO" workflow and a mandatory git-commit-and-push end-session checklist.
> Both have been removed because they conflict with common workflows (notably
> "never commit without explicit permission," which is the safer default).

## Use the brain before guessing

You have persistent transcript memory across every session. Use it.

- Before proposing a plan: search the brain for prior decisions on the same topic
- Before debugging: search the brain for error messages or symptoms
- Tools: `search_transcripts`, `search_semantic`, `get_recent_summaries`,
  `lookup_decision`, `lookup_fact`, `get_profile`, `get_project_state`
- Not using available tools is the same as not having them

## Version-control caution

The default in this template is **don't commit code without explicit
permission**. Read-only git (status / log / diff / fetch / branch / checkout)
is fine. Mutations (commit / push / add / reset / force-push) require an
explicit ask. Override if your workflow needs auto-commit, but be deliberate.

## End-of-session, when meaningful

If the session produced load-bearing decisions or notes worth keeping, write
them to the brain:

```bash
python3 scripts/write_session_notes.py --notes "<text>" --tags "<tag1,tag2>"
```

The `session-end.py` hook will also auto-suggest tags from transcript content
if you don't supply them. Both paths are fine — explicit beats implicit when
the session mattered.

## Replace this section with your own rules

Examples to consider:
- Project-specific conventions (test command, lint command, build command)
- Forbidden patterns (don't touch the legacy auth flow, don't write to prod)
- Tools you prefer (Edit over Write, Grep over `grep` subprocess, etc.)
- Identity-level notes ("call me by my preferred name," etc.)
